hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  in the ethical writings that we still  possess by Epicurus we see one of the  main themes being echoed over and over  again that there are certain things that  human beings do tend to fear that it's  really not good or prudent for us to  fear and one of the things that he wants  to do with your philosophical argument  is to lead us away from these fears this  is one of the things that philosophy in  his view can actually provide to us so  when we think about fear what is fear in  this this epicurean perspective fear is  a mental disturbance it's it's a feeling  it's a passion and as a passion that  causes us some pain or some grief it  disturbs us it keeps us from being  tranquil we're never quite at home with  ourselves when we're fearing we can't  enjoy the things that we actually have  when work when we're inhabited by fear  sometimes we actually do turn to  pleasures to try to mask fear or to  distract ourselves from fear but  Epicurus like I put here he thinks that  fear is actually worse than physical  pains and so it's mental pains and  pleasures have a predominance over  physical pains and pleasures for him the  attempts to try to compensate by  indulging ourselves and pleasures not  really going to address our fear so how  can we really address our fears how can  we become free of them well we have to  first of all be conscious of what we're  actually afraid of and in his time he  identified two things in particular or  two sets of things in particular one of  them is an odd thing according to him  that people tended to focus on one of  them is the gods or fate or the workings  of the universe and the natural world  and the other is death  now how applicable is this to our own  time well you know we're all going to  die the mortality rate for human beings  at least in a physical sense is a  hundred percent in any given population  right sooner or later all physical life  ceases there are people who are you know  working on this anti-death you know sort  of idea but but that's at this point  just sort of science fiction we are not  in world as a matter of fact even the  gods are not necessarily immortal from  an epicurean point of view there they're  just different kinds of beings what  about the gods or fate do we still have  concerns about that sort of thing where  are many religious believers they are  not you know practicing for the most  part the same religion that Epicurus was  had in mind the polytheistic common  persons view on the gods as a matter of  fact Epicurus says those people are they  think they're pious but they're actually  impious because they don't know the true  nature of the gods and they by you know  they do all these prayers and have all  these fears about the gods there are a  lot of people who whether we you know  change it and make it a monotheistic  thing have something identifiable with  what what he's talking about and there  are other people who tend to view the  universe as a necessarily hostile place  inhabited by you know some sort of you  know meanness towards human beings or  indifference towards human beings  there are some people who believe in a  kind of determinism that we cannot  escape from  so Epicurus it is you know addressing  these sorts of concerns let's look first  in his his views on the gods and this is  the views not only about the curious but  of all of his school that's going to  follow him first of all  against some of the other philosophical  schools of antiquity Epicurus asserted  that there actually is human freedom  that maybe some things follow by natural  processes and causal orders as a matter  of fact he had a whole you know sort of  atomic theory that that fits in with  this but there's also the possibility of  freedom and we experience this so we  don't have to be concerned that our  destiny has been cast in stone and we  can't escape it we are to some degree  responsible for ourselves we can choose  which path we go on we have some control  over our own lives what about the gods  Epicurus didn't believe in the gods he  says most people have got them wrong the  gods are these perfectly blissful higher  beings and because they're perfectly  blissful they have nothing to do with  human beings or human affairs  they don't take an interest they don't  you know play favorites they don't get  involved in our Wars like I put here  gods don't get angry they don't become  envious the ancient Greeks you know  worried quite a bit about making the  gods envious if you get too successful  maybe the gods are gonna stick it to you  because they're they don't like it when  you're like the gods  Epicurus says no look there were totally  blissful beings they enjoy a higher  state of existence than us and if they  actually did get involved with us  there's two things that we could say one  is they no longer enjoyed the kind of  happiness that they do enjoy right  because they'd be bound up with all of  our craziness down here but the other  thing is to be able to get angry to be  upset about things is in Epicurus view a  sign of a kind of imperfection a  weakness  the part and so if we if we think of the  gods as being these maximal beings they  wouldn't have that as part of what they  are so what this means for us is not  only are we free but the gods aren't  going to mess around with us and  interfere with us as a matter of fact we  can look at the gods as being sort of  examples of what we should aspire to  natural phenomena the Greeks were and  the Romans were very concerned as many  people were about the connection between  natural phenomena and the will of God or  the gods  Epicurus ins they have nothing to do  with each other earthquakes happen for  natural causes hail storms happen and  destroy crops by natural causes we can  actually do natural science or what he  called physics at the time and we can  make sense of this if you get sick it's  because some disease organism as we now  say all right there's a lot been a lot  of theories of medicine about what  causes disease like the asthma and  things like that but you know if you get  cancer according to Epicurus it's not  because god has it in for you or god is  trying to punish you or you did  something wrong and guys punishing if  it's natural causes and we have the  freedom to study these things and to  make sense of them and if we do go on  and study these things a lot of  epicurean philosophy was indeed about  trying to make sense of meteorological  phenomena we will enjoy life more  because we will understand the universe  that will become intelligible to us it  will no longer be a source of fear but  rather a source of confidence and even  pleasure what about this though dad we  can't avoid death as a matter of fact  Epicurus says everybody who comes in  this world is moral and there's no way  around that so you know don't live as if  you're going to live forever live in a  prudent way so that you can actually get  the most out of this life he also thinks  this is going to be talked about a  little bit later in a different video  that the old person who is close to  death can  in fact have the most pleasant life  because they have this entire stock of  memories that they can look back on  death puts a limit to that though but  what is death according to Epicurus  death is simply the cessation of  sensation we no longer sense anything we  no longer perceive and indeed of  existence there it's not as if I lose my  existence as if existence was a property  according to Epicurus there's no longer  any me to lose anything there  there's no immortal soul there's no you  know mind that lasts beyond the body for  Epicurus this is what you get what you  see is what you get literally for him  right so this actually introduces a kind  of dilemma to us but it's a happy  dilemma  according to Epicurus when can we  actually be afraid of death we can be  afraid of death as something coming in  the future which then we would fear when  we're alive and Epicurus says it's  really silly for you to be fearing death  then because it ain't here Yeah right if  you're if death is in the future why are  you concerned about something that is  happening down the line but it's not yet  present so while we're living we don't  need to fear death because we are living  what about when we hit that point  well it's no longer a point for us when  we hit it because there's no us there's  no there's nothing beyond that for the  being that is experiencing death as a  matter of fact you cannot according to  Epicurus experience your death or suffer  death because when you die when death  comes that's the end of that person the  person no longer has existence that is  what it means so if you shouldn't fear  death right now because there's nothing  to actually fear because it's far off  and you shouldn't fear death because  when it happens there won't be a you to  actually suffer it the the conclusion of  that is that it's a groundless fear from  ourselves of death now why might  somebody actually be afraid  you know they could be concerned about  their legacy  they could be concerned about the people  that they're leaving behind they could  be concerned about you know the house  that they're abandoning they could also  be worried about an afterlife in which  the gods are going to punish them for  being such jerks or the gods will punish  them because they're jerks are something  along those lines but Epicurus has  already cut off that possibility and if  we have our our way of living straight  there's not going to be anything that we  need to worry about after we die  interestingly Epicurus one of the things  that we do have preserved from him in  Diogenes leontes is his last will and  testament he actually did leave a legacy  he established that philosophical school  the garden and he laid down provisions  for how it ought to run including the  celebration of his birthday which was  you know celebrated the way that many  people will celebrate you know religious  figures birthdays as well right but you  know what is he ultimately concerned  about that would he allow himself to get  upset if he thought or feared that  things might not come to pass the way  that he ordained them no those things  are not in his control and even if they  they didn't go that way there's no  Epicurus left anymore to be bothered by  it it's not like there's Hades where you  can be looking up and say oh look at  what they did with the lawn you know  that's not going to happen in that case  so Epicurus thinks that if we can  eliminate these groundless fears we will  in fact have opened up the the path to  happiness for ourselves
 

